In the photon picture, light of frequency f is not a continuous wave but a stream of discrete energy packets called PHOTONS. Each photon has:
- energy E = h f = h c/lambda;
- momentum p = E/c = h/lambda, even though its rest mass is zero;
- no electric charge, so it is undeflected by electric or magnetic fields;
- speed c in vacuum.
The number of photons in a beam is proportional to its intensity, and in any interaction with matter the total energy and momentum are conserved.

Dual nature: some phenomena (interference, diffraction, polarisation) are explained only by treating light as a WAVE, while others (photoelectric effect, Compton effect) are explained only by treating light as a stream of PARTICLES (photons). Neither description alone is complete; light exhibits a wave nature in propagation and a particle nature in its interaction with matter. This combined behaviour is called the dual nature of radiation.
